1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:03,900
Imagine sending a birthday cake across town.

2
00:00:05,150 --> 00:00:08,600
But the delivery system records the wrong address.

3
00:00:10,300 --> 00:00:13,000
Someone confirms it without noticing.

4
00:00:13,300 --> 00:00:14,600
Confirmed!

5
00:00:15,200 --> 00:00:18,300
Now everything runs beautifully.

6
00:00:18,300 --> 00:00:21,700
Sorted. Dispatched. Right on schedule.

7
00:00:23,400 --> 00:00:25,300
Delivery successful!

8
00:00:27,800 --> 00:00:30,000
The delivery was confirmed.

9
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:32,000
The address was still wrong.

10
00:00:32,000 --> 00:00:34,800
What happens when an interface makes the same mistake?

11
00:00:36,160 --> 00:00:39,980
A conversation can feel successful even when the information

12
00:00:39,980 --> 00:00:42,640
is wrong. That is the question behind our project:

13
00:00:43,040 --> 00:00:46,840
when collecting structured information, when does chat help, and

14
00:00:46,840 --> 00:00:49,660
when is a traditional form a better choice?

15
00:00:50,140 --> 00:00:54,865
We are Team C-10, teamGARI: Raja Suleman, Raj Lakhani,

16
00:00:54,865 --> 00:00:57,967
Awais Basheer, and Muhammad Immad.

17
00:00:57,967 --> 00:01:01,040
Our task is setting up a study-planner profile with

18
00:01:01,040 --> 00:01:03,520
courses, exam dates, and work evenings.

19
00:01:03,520 --> 00:01:07,580
We compare the two interfaces through three questions: how

20
00:01:07,580 --> 00:01:10,700
long does the interaction take, what information gets confirmed,

21
00:01:10,700 --> 00:01:14,680
and how easily can the user check and correct

22
00:01:14,680 --> 00:01:15,060
it?

23
00:01:16,100 --> 00:01:20,300
Before the demonstration, one important distinction: our chat is

24
00:01:20,300 --> 00:01:22,900
a scripted simulation, not a live language model.

25
00:01:23,440 --> 00:01:27,040
Its mistakes were deliberately programmed to illustrate possible failure

26
00:01:27,040 --> 00:01:30,760
modes. Our results come from one team member’s run,

27
00:01:30,590 --> 00:01:34,360
so they are illustrative, not a validated comparison.

28
00:01:36,460 --> 00:01:38,900
Here are the two versions of our prototype: the

29
00:01:38,900 --> 00:01:40,680
form on the left and the scripted chat on

30
00:01:40,680 --> 00:01:43,740
the right. In the first scenario, the student has

31
00:01:43,740 --> 00:01:48,660
two exams next week, for CS3014 and MT1003, and

32
00:01:48,660 --> 00:01:50,880
works Tuesday and Thursday evenings.

33
00:01:50,880 --> 00:01:54,420
In chat, the message produces a summary showing the

34
00:01:54,420 --> 00:01:57,380
courses, the exam week, and the work evenings.

35
00:01:57,380 --> 00:02:00,180
The tester confirms it, and the displayed timer stops

36
00:02:00,180 --> 00:02:03,740
at 8.6 seconds. On the form, the tester selects

37
00:02:03,740 --> 00:02:07,620
the courses, enters individual exam dates, and chooses the

38
00:02:07,620 --> 00:02:11,480
work evenings. The displayed timer stops at 12.5 seconds.

39
00:02:12,060 --> 00:02:15,440
Both complete this scenario without the planted mistakes.

40
00:02:16,080 --> 00:02:19,320
However, the outputs differ in detail: chat records the

41
00:02:19,320 --> 00:02:21,600
week, while the form records exact dates.

42
00:02:22,280 --> 00:02:24,740
Keep that difference in mind when comparing them.

43
00:02:24,894 --> 00:02:27,974
Now the input is less precise: “Exams are after

44
00:02:27,974 --> 00:02:30,454
the break, I’m in CS 3041, and I work

45
00:02:30,804 --> 00:02:32,634
Mondays.” There is no exact exam date.

46
00:02:33,354 --> 00:02:36,214
The course code is also mistyped: the intended code

47
00:02:36,214 --> 00:02:39,054
is CS3014. Watch the chat summary.

48
00:02:39,494 --> 00:02:42,094
It supplies the twenty-second of November, even though the

49
00:02:42,094 --> 00:02:44,194
message never gave that date.

50
00:02:44,834 --> 00:02:47,174
It also carries the incorrect course code into the

51
00:02:47,174 --> 00:02:50,494
summary. The tester highlights the date, but still selects

52
00:02:50,494 --> 00:02:54,514
Confirm. The interface displays “Profile saved,” with both incorrect

53
00:02:54,514 --> 00:02:57,754
values accepted. This is a programmed demonstration of the

54
00:02:57,754 --> 00:03:01,394
failure, not an observed language-model prediction.

55
00:03:01,394 --> 00:03:05,554
The design question it illustrates is still useful: does

56
00:03:05,554 --> 00:03:08,694
a confirmation button help if the user misses what

57
00:03:08,494 --> 00:03:09,274
needs correcting?

58
00:03:09,807 --> 00:03:13,327
On the form, the tester leaves the required exam

59
00:03:13,327 --> 00:03:16,987
date and work evenings blank, then tries to save.

60
00:03:17,227 --> 00:03:19,587
An error summary identifies the two missing entries.

61
00:03:20,227 --> 00:03:22,867
The tester supplies the date, selects Monday, and saves

62
00:03:22,867 --> 00:03:26,467
successfully. The displayed timer stops at 18.7 seconds.

63
00:03:26,927 --> 00:03:30,607
The form catches missing required information in this run.

64
00:03:30,607 --> 00:03:32,847
That does not mean it can detect every wrong

65
00:03:32,847 --> 00:03:35,927
answer. The contrast is that this form demands an

66
00:03:35,927 --> 00:03:39,127
explicit value, while our scripted chat substitutes a date

67
00:03:39,467 --> 00:03:40,327
and accepts the typo.

68
00:03:43,480 --> 00:03:45,341
What do these results actually tell us?

69
00:03:46,020 --> 00:03:49,660
First, time. The displayed chat times were 8.6 seconds

70
00:03:49,660 --> 00:03:53,380
in scenario one and 13 seconds in scenario two.

71
00:03:53,380 --> 00:03:56,820
The form times were 12.5 and 18.7 seconds.

72
00:03:57,260 --> 00:04:00,100
However, the timers start at different points.

73
00:04:00,540 --> 00:04:03,940
The form starts timing at the first input; chat

74
00:04:03,940 --> 00:04:07,580
starts when the message is sent, excluding message preparation.

75
00:04:07,580 --> 00:04:11,440
The interfaces also collect different levels of date detail.

76
00:04:12,061 --> 00:04:15,561
These are demonstration timings, not a controlled test proving

77
00:04:15,561 --> 00:04:19,200
that chat is faster. Second, errors.

78
00:04:19,760 --> 00:04:22,721
In the messy scenario, the chat accepts two incorrect

79
00:04:22,721 --> 00:04:25,841
values, while the form blocks missing entries.

80
00:04:27,001 --> 00:04:30,341
Third, control. A summary gives users an opportunity to

81
00:04:30,341 --> 00:04:33,781
review, but simply showing information does not guarantee that

82
00:04:33,781 --> 00:04:34,821
mistakes will be noticed.

83
00:04:37,854 --> 00:04:39,934
Our slides also bring in published research.

84
00:04:40,514 --> 00:04:42,874
The studies we cite report that a single-page form

85
00:04:42,874 --> 00:04:46,054
was fastest in one comparison, and that a chatbot

86
00:04:46,054 --> 00:04:48,874
took longer in another. The cited work by Wei

87
00:04:48,874 --> 00:04:52,214
and colleagues examines how well a language-model chatbot collects

88
00:04:52,214 --> 00:04:55,834
target information, including the risk of leaving information out.

89
00:04:56,054 --> 00:04:58,834
That is a different measure from simply counting wrong

90
00:04:58,834 --> 00:05:02,474
values. These studies use different tasks and systems, so

91
00:05:02,384 --> 00:05:05,074
their results should not be treated as one universal

92
00:05:05,074 --> 00:05:08,934
ranking. Our demonstration illustrates a design problem.

93
00:05:09,274 --> 00:05:11,714
It does not establish how often real chatbots fail,

94
00:05:11,754 --> 00:05:14,154
or overturn findings from larger studies.

95
00:05:14,428 --> 00:05:17,148
There are important limits to our work.

96
00:05:17,328 --> 00:05:20,528
We built the prototype ourselves, tested it with one

97
00:05:20,528 --> 00:05:23,728
team member, and deliberately scripted the chat’s mistakes.

98
00:05:24,428 --> 00:05:28,028
We cannot generalise these results to all users or

99
00:05:28,028 --> 00:05:32,208
to real language models. Our keyboard-only pass also found

100
00:05:32,208 --> 00:05:37,628
problems in both interfaces. In chat, reaching Confirm required

101
00:05:37,628 --> 00:05:40,948
moving through other controls because focus was not moved

102
00:05:40,948 --> 00:05:43,928
to the new summary. In the form, we encountered

103
00:05:43,928 --> 00:05:47,808
difficulty dismissing the browser’s date picker with the keyboard.

104
00:05:48,328 --> 00:05:51,388
That behaviour may depend on the browser and platform.

105
00:05:52,148 --> 00:05:56,548
These observations show why accessibility needs its own evaluation.

106
00:05:57,448 --> 00:05:59,848
A visually clear interface is not enough.

107
00:06:00,081 --> 00:06:02,541
We did not test with a screen reader.

108
00:06:02,541 --> 00:06:06,381
The prototype includes a conversation log role, but that

109
00:06:06,381 --> 00:06:09,181
alone does not verify that new replies are announced

110
00:06:09,181 --> 00:06:12,521
correctly. We would need to test announcements, focus order,

111
00:06:12,521 --> 00:06:15,081
and correction controls with assistive technology.

112
00:06:16,041 --> 00:06:18,701
We also distinguish research evidence from marketing.

113
00:06:19,481 --> 00:06:22,781
The Typeform completion claim discussed in our slides is

114
00:06:22,771 --> 00:06:26,761
a secondhand vendor claim about conversational forms, not evidence

115
00:06:26,761 --> 00:06:28,701
that language-model chat is better.

116
00:06:28,701 --> 00:06:30,641
We did not test it.

117
00:06:31,061 --> 00:06:34,221
Finally, free text can invite people to share more

118
00:06:34,221 --> 00:06:37,841
information than necessary. If a future version sends messages

119
00:06:37,841 --> 00:06:42,141
to an external service, that creates additional privacy considerations.

120
00:06:42,881 --> 00:06:47,321
Our demonstration uses fake data and a local scripted

121
00:06:47,848 --> 00:06:48,855
parser.

122
00:06:49,834 --> 00:06:53,114
Our recommendation is to start with the information the

123
00:06:53,114 --> 00:06:56,114
task requires. When the fields are known and correctness

124
00:06:56,114 --> 00:06:59,534
matters, a form makes those requirements visible.

125
00:06:59,614 --> 00:07:02,514
Chat can help people express messy or narrative information,

126
00:07:02,954 --> 00:07:06,594
but uncertainty needs to stay visible, and corrections need

127
00:07:06,594 --> 00:07:10,114
to be easy. For this task, we propose a

128
00:07:10,114 --> 00:07:13,854
hybrid: let free text pre-fill a form, then let

129
00:07:13,854 --> 00:07:16,394
the user inspect and edit the fields before confirming.

130
00:07:16,394 --> 00:07:19,414
Ask for missing dates instead of guessing them, and

131
00:07:19,414 --> 00:07:22,414
flag an unrecognised course code.

132
00:07:22,874 --> 00:07:25,314
This is our proposed improvement, not a feature we

133
00:07:25,314 --> 00:07:28,634
tested. For our project, this informs the D3 and

134
00:07:28,634 --> 00:07:31,934
D4 input-flow design and the D5 accessibility evaluation.

135
00:07:32,634 --> 00:07:35,494
Next, we would test with real users, use consistent

136
00:07:35,494 --> 00:07:39,254
timing rules, check screen-reader behaviour, and measure errors across

137
00:07:39,254 --> 00:07:41,894
repeated runs. The key question is not just whether

138
00:07:41,894 --> 00:07:45,154
the interaction feels natural. It is whether people can

139
00:07:45,154 --> 00:07:47,854
understand and control what the system records.

140
00:07:48,254 --> 00:07:49,014
Thank you for watching.
